The PIR signal is an input to the Uno. The buzzer control pin is an output from the Uno. Because this buzzer module is explicitly LOW-triggered, writing LOW turns it on and writing HIGH turns it off.

```cpp
const int pirPin = 2;
const int buzzerPin = 9;

void setup() {
  pinMode(pirPin, INPUT);
  pinMode(buzzerPin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(buzzerPin, HIGH);  // LOW-trigger module: start OFF
}

void loop() {
  int motion = digitalRead(pirPin);

  if (motion == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(buzzerPin, LOW);   // ON
  } else {
    digitalWrite(buzzerPin, HIGH);  // OFF
  }
}
```

Do not invert the module behavior by assuming that HIGH means ON. The module's stated trigger polarity controls the required output logic.

