Wiring:
- DS18B20 module VCC -> ESP32 3V3.
- DS18B20 module GND -> ESP32 GND.
- DS18B20 module DATA -> ESP32 GPIO18.
- ESP32 GPIO23 -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode.
- LED cathode -> ESP32 GND.

Because this DS18B20 module already has its DATA pull-up resistor, do not add another pull-up. Powering the module from 3.3 V also keeps its DATA pull-up at a safe 3.3 V logic level for the ESP32.

```cpp
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

const int ONE_WIRE_PIN = 18;
const int LED_PIN = 23;

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_PIN);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  sensors.begin();
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();
  float temperatureC = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);

  if (temperatureC == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, temperatureC >= 28.0 ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

The DS18B20 DATA line is bidirectional one-wire communication, so GPIO18 is used by the library for both transmitting protocol timing and reading the sensor response. The LED path remains a separate GPIO output and includes the required series resistor.

Do not treat -127 C as a real measured temperature. With the DallasTemperature library, a value equal to DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C (commonly -127 C) indicates that a valid temperature was not obtained from the sensor.

Check the physical path before changing application thresholds:

1. Power off the circuit.
2. Confirm the module VCC is connected to ESP32 3V3.
3. Confirm the module GND is connected to ESP32 GND.
4. Confirm the module DATA pin is connected to GPIO18, not to VCC or GND.
5. Because this is a module with an onboard 4.7 kOhm pull-up, do not add another pull-up unless the actual module documentation shows that one is absent.
6. Inspect loose jumpers, breadboard contact, and the module pin labels. Do not assume a bare-sensor TO-92 pin order applies to a breakout module.
7. Restore power and first verify that the 1-Wire bus can discover a device.

A minimal diagnostic can check the device count and reject a disconnected reading:

```cpp
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

const int ONE_WIRE_PIN = 18;

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_PIN);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  sensors.begin();

  Serial.print("Devices found: ");
  Serial.println(sensors.getDeviceCount());
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();
  float t = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);

  if (t == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    Serial.println("DS18B20 read failed: check power, ground, DATA, and bus connection.");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(t);
    Serial.println(" C");
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

If the device count is zero or the reading remains DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C, keep troubleshooting the sensor connection rather than using -127 C in control logic. Only apply the low-temperature alarm rule after a valid reading has been confirmed.

An exact 85.0 C reading immediately after startup can be the DS18B20 power-on/default scratchpad value rather than a completed temperature conversion. Do not treat the first 85.0 C automatically as a confirmed over-temperature event.

Keep the existing wiring:
- DS18B20 module VCC -> ESP32 3V3.
- DS18B20 module GND -> ESP32 GND.
- DS18B20 module DATA -> ESP32 GPIO18.
- Keep the module's onboard 4.7 kOhm DATA pull-up; do not add another pull-up.

Use an explicit conversion request before reading the temperature:

```cpp
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

const int ONE_WIRE_PIN = 18;

OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_PIN);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  sensors.begin();
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();
  float t = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);

  if (t == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C) {
    Serial.println("Sensor read failed");
  } else if (t == 85.0f) {
    Serial.println("85 C received: verify with another completed conversion before alarming");
  } else {
    Serial.print("Temperature: ");
    Serial.print(t);
    Serial.println(" C");

    if (t >= 80.0f) {
      Serial.println("OVER-TEMPERATURE ALARM");
    }
  }

  delay(1000);
}
```

With the DallasTemperature library's normal blocking behavior, requestTemperatures() starts the conversion and waits for it to complete before getTempCByIndex(0) reads the result. If 85.0 C appears only at startup and the next completed conversion is normal, treat the first value as unconfirmed. If repeated completed conversions continue to report about 85 C, it may be a real high temperature and should not be discarded merely because the value equals 85.0.
